### TRUST THROUGH VISUAL DESIGN (Weinschenk #79)

Research shows that 83% of users judge a product's trustworthiness based on visual design BEFORE evaluating content or functionality. Look-and-feel is the **first trust gate**.

**Practical rules**:
- **First impression budget**: Invest disproportionate design effort in the first screen a user sees — landing page, login, or dashboard. If this screen looks amateur, users will not trust the product regardless of features.
- **Professional signals**: Consistent spacing, refined typography, restrained color, and high-quality imagery signal competence. These are not aesthetic preferences — they are trust indicators.
- **Trust hierarchy**: Visual polish > testimonials > feature descriptions. Users decide to stay or leave in 50ms based on visual quality.
- **The implication for MVP/early products**: Even a minimal product needs visual coherence. A polished 3-screen flow beats a rough 20-screen flow for trust-building.

---

### CANONICAL PERSPECTIVE AND OBJECT RECOGNITION (Weinschenk #3, #5)

The brain identifies objects through pattern recognition (geons — simple 3D shapes) and expects objects shown from a slightly-above, slightly-tilted angle (canonical perspective).

**Practical rules for product/illustration design**:
- **Product images**: Show objects at a 3/4 angle, slightly above — this is the angle users expect and process fastest
- **Icons**: Use the most recognizable angle of an object — a house from the front, a phone tilted slightly, a document face-on
- **3D illustrations**: Prefer canonical angles over dramatic perspectives — faster recognition, less cognitive effort

---

### PERIPHERAL VISION AND SCREEN SCANNING (Weinschenk #2, #6)

Users rely on peripheral vision to grasp overall page structure BEFORE reading details. Scanning follows learned patterns (F-pattern on web, center-weighted on mobile).

**Practical rules**:
- **Page edges matter less**: Users rarely fixate on the extreme edges of a screen. Do not place critical actions at the far periphery.
- **Structural landmarks**: Headers, section dividers, and whitespace create the peripheral "map" users scan. Without these, the page feels like a wall of undifferentiated content.
- **Change blindness** (#8): Users often miss changes in areas they are not actively looking at. When content updates dynamically (notifications, status changes), use motion or color flash to draw attention — a silent update may go unnoticed entirely.

